     3	## What is proven
     4	
     5	The preserved `solution.py` translates byte-for-byte to `solution.mpy` and
     6	executes to the expected alternating path on two fixed-semantics witnesses
     7	covering odd and even `k` (and one and two loop iterations).  K proves these
     8	auxiliary reachability claims with `#Top`:
     9	
    10	- the two inner-scan invariants and two outer-scan invariants together;
    11	- the source-level nested-scan completion claim;
    12	- the exact four-conditional neighbor computation claim; and
    13	- the generalized result-loop claim for a symbolic positive remaining count
    14	  and a symbolic accumulated prefix.
    15	
    16	The required `minpath-full-contract` claim over arbitrary `N >= 2`, arbitrary
    17	permutation grids, and arbitrary positive `K` does not close.  No successful
    18	proof of the full HumanEval contract is reported.
    19	
    20	## Formal claim
    21	
    22	`minpath-full-contract` starts from the exact call to the translated
    23	`minPath` closure.  Its precondition represents every square `N >= 2` grid
    24	whose flattened integer sequence is a permutation of `1..N*N`, and every
    25	`K > 0`.  Its postcondition requires the returned list to satisfy
    26	`finishRel(.ValSeq, OUT, (K - pyMod(K,2))/2, neighborMin(...), K)`, i.e. the
    27	sequence `1, M, 1, M, ...`, truncated to length `K`, where `M` is the minimum
    28	orthogonal neighbor of the unique cell containing `1`.
    29	
    30	This sequence is the intended lexicographic minimum: every minimum path must
    31	start at the unique global minimum `1`; its least possible second value is the
    32	least adjacent value `M`; returning to `1` is then least, and the argument
    33	repeats.
    34	
